    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_2e3cb030a92b0f566c0cb12215e6c8fca75bc24bcaa58faa2bf86424a0719f93",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The first-derivative argument and the higher-derivative inheritance argument share a precise sign-control strategy but realize it differently: functional-equation pairing makes Re(ζ′/ζ) negative on selected boundaries for contour counting, whereas a partial-fraction expansion of ζ^(m+1)/ζ^(m) separates zero populations so finitely many left nonreal exceptions stay bounded and the infinite regularly spaced real-zero tail forces eventual negativity. The latter converts the earlier boundary-sign idea into an inductive nonvanishing mechanism for subsequent derivatives.",
