    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_aeeef363c1f4bac254fd19577909bed57a7068f7b49bf751fac3be32f54d07d1",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Littlewood's lemma first turns the normalized kth derivative's boundary growth into both a weighted horizontal zero sum and an unweighted zero count. Subtracting the count removes total mass and isolates signed displacement from the critical line, while a separate critical-line mean bound controls total displacement strongly enough to convert the same boundary statistic into two-sided clustering, including the stated short-interval regime.",
